/** Generated Markdown guidance derived from kind Metadata. */
export const chartKindAuthorGuide = "# Built-in Chart kinds\n\n## Bar (`bar`)\n\nNamed categories compared by bar length from a zero baseline, side by side or as proportions of each category's whole.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Comparing measured magnitudes across a small set of named categories.\n- Showing how each category's whole divides across series as proportions.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The subject is a trend over a continuous scale; the line kind serves it.\n- Values are negative; the diverging bar variant is deferred and negative values are refused.\n- The reader needs identity and topology rather than quantities; use a diagram kind.\n\nBudgets:\n- series: 6 series. Maximum simultaneous series, matching the fixed palette slots; grouped bars read best at four or fewer. Remedy: reduce-series.\n- categories: 12 categories. Maximum named categories in one readable figure. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- categoryLabelGraphemes: 48 graphemes. Maximum concise category label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- seriesLabelGraphemes: 32 graphemes. Maximum concise series label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- valueMagnitudeSpan: 4 decades. Maximum decimal orders separating the smallest and largest nonzero value magnitudes; bar length keeps its zero baseline, so the log scale lives in the position-encoding kinds. Remedy: log-scale.\n\n## Line (`line`)\n\nOne or more measured series followed through an ordered numeric or date domain, with an optional single-series area fill from the zero baseline.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Following how a measured value moves through an ordered domain such as time.\n- Comparing a small set of series over the same ordered domain.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The domain is a set of named categories rather than an ordered scale; the bar kind serves it.\n- The reader needs the shape and spread of repeated measurements; the distribution kind serves it.\n- Several series should stack into one filled total; small multiples or the table serve it, and the area variant stays single-series.\n\nBudgets:\n- series: 6 series. Maximum simultaneous series, matching the fixed palette slots; crossing paths read best at three or fewer. Remedy: reduce-series.\n- points: 120 points. Maximum ordered domain positions in one figure; every authored point renders without resampling, so a longer series splits rather than decimates. Remedy: split-figure.\n- seriesLabelGraphemes: 32 graphemes. Maximum concise series label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- valueMagnitudeSpan: 6 decades. Maximum decimal orders separating the smallest and largest positive value magnitudes under the linear scale; the log scale carries wider spans honestly, so the budget does not bind there. Remedy: log-scale.\n\n## Distribution (`distribution`)\n\nThe shape and spread of repeated measurements: a histogram over author-declared or rule-named bin edges, or a box five-number summary of the same recorded values.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Showing how one set of repeated measurements spreads across its range.\n- Summarising a sample's centre and spread with the five Tukey numbers.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Each value belongs to a named category; the bar kind compares them.\n- The subject is a trend over an ordered scale; the line kind serves it.\n- Every recorded value is equal; a table or a single stated value is honest.\n\nBudgets:\n- values: 2000 values. Maximum recorded measurements in one figure; exact decimal binning and quartile derivation stay documentation-scale, and a larger sample reads better split by cohort. Remedy: split-figure.\n- bins: 20 bins. Maximum histogram bins; every bin prints its full range and count on every surface, and more rows stop reading as one figure. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- valueLabelGraphemes: 32 graphemes. Maximum concise measured-quantity axis label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n\n## Heatmap (`heatmap`)\n\nOne measured quantity read across a grid of two category sets, encoded as declared bins on the sequential accent ramp.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Reading how one magnitude distributes across every pairing of two category sets.\n- Spotting concentrated and sparse regions in a matrix, such as activity by weekday and daypart.\n\nDo not use when:\n- The cells are boolean capabilities or categorical facts rather than one measured quantity; Table and ComparisonTable serve those.\n- The reader needs finer distinction than four declared bins honestly carry; re-bin with new declared edges or present the exact values as a table.\n- The subject is one series' trend over time rather than a two-way grid; the line kind serves it.\n\nBudgets:\n- rows: 20 rows. Maximum named rows one grid can carry while every row label stays legible beside its cells. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- columns: 14 columns. Maximum named columns in one readable grid; column labels sit above narrow cells, so columns cap tighter than rows. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- bins: 4 bins. Maximum declared bins, matching the four sequential ramp slots where lightness carries the value; a scale needing more must re-bin with new declared edges rather than dither. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- rowLabelGraphemes: 24 graphemes. Maximum concise row label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- columnLabelGraphemes: 12 graphemes. Maximum concise column label length; column labels sit above narrow cells. Remedy: shorten-label.\n\n## Scatter (`scatter`)\n\nObserved (x, y) pairs positioned on two quantitative scales so the reader judges the relationship between the two quantities.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Judging how two measured quantities relate across many observations.\n- Showing where observations cluster, spread, or stand apart on two scales.\n\nDo not use when:\n- One axis is an ordered progression the reader follows; the line kind serves it.\n- A third quantity would need to become bubble size; size encoding is refused, so split the figure instead.\n- The reader compares named categories rather than paired quantities; the bar kind serves it.\n\nBudgets:\n- series: 3 series. Maximum simultaneous series, matching the three paired colour-plus-marker bundles (circle, square, triangle); a fourth population would have to differ by colour alone. Remedy: reduce-series.\n- pointsPerSeries: 200 points. Maximum observations one series may state; past this the exact-pair table stops being a readable document and overplotting hides structure. Remedy: split-figure.\n- seriesLabelGraphemes: 32 graphemes. Maximum concise series label length. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- xMagnitudeSpan: 6 decades. Maximum decimal orders separating the smallest and largest nonzero x magnitudes on a linear scale; position encodes the value here, so the log scale is the honest remedy. Remedy: log-scale.\n- yMagnitudeSpan: 6 decades. Maximum decimal orders separating the smallest and largest nonzero y magnitudes on a linear scale; position encodes the value here, so the log scale is the honest remedy. Remedy: log-scale.\n\n## Slope (`slope`)\n\nEach named item's before and after compared as one connecting line between two vertical ordinal axes, with direct labels instead of a legend. The terminal form is permanently a textual delta list — label, both authored values, a direction triangle, and the exact signed delta — not a drawing.\n\nCLI stance: enhanced.\n\nUse when:\n- Comparing how each of several named items moved between two named states or moments.\n- Showing which items rose, which fell, and which held level across one shared change.\n\nDo not use when:\n- Only one item is compared; a Stat with a trend slot serves a single before/after pair.\n- Each item carries more than two measured moments; the line kind serves a trend.\n- The reader compares magnitudes from a zero baseline; the bar kind's grouped variant serves that.\n\nBudgets:\n- items: 12 items. Maximum named items in one readable figure; each item carries two direct labels, so the ceiling matches the bar kind's category ceiling. Remedy: aggregate-categories.\n- itemLabelGraphemes: 32 graphemes. Maximum concise item label length; the label prints beside the plot on every surface. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- endpointLabelGraphemes: 16 graphemes. Maximum endpoint name length; both names caption an axis and head a terminal column. Remedy: shorten-label.\n- valueMagnitudeSpan: 4 decades. Maximum decimal orders separating the smallest and largest nonzero value magnitudes; slope stays linear in v1, so wide spans split into focused figures. Remedy: split-figure.\n\n## Refused forms\n\n- Pie and donut charts — Author the bar kind's proportion variant instead.\n- Radar charts — Refused: axes at arbitrary angles cannot be read honestly.\n- Gauges — Use the Meter Component, which owns the capacity posture.\n- Bubble charts — Refused: area encoding misstates magnitude.\n- Dual value axes — Author two figures, or index both series to a common base.\n- Stacked areas — Author small multiples, or present the table.\n- 3D charts — Refused: projected depth distorts every comparison.\n- Streaming or animated charts — Refused: the chart library is static by decision.\n";
